<TopicPage slug="subjects/cybersecurity/foundational/security-foundations-the-cia-triad">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Most engineering disciplines fight nature: gravity, friction, entropy, weather.
    Cybersecurity fights an adversary — intelligent, adaptive, and actively trying to defeat
    you. That one difference reshapes everything. A bridge does not study its designer's
    habits and look for the unguarded afternoon; an attacker does. The discipline's answers
    are correspondingly distinctive: layered defences that fail gracefully, controls justified
    by the cost of the incidents they prevent, and a shared professional vocabulary precise
    enough that a hospital's security team, its auditors and its board can reason about the
    same threat without talking past each other.
  </p>
  <p>
    The vocabulary has two pillars. The first is the <strong>CIA triad</strong> —
    confidentiality, integrity, availability — the three properties every security decision
    protects, trades off or sacrifices. The second is <strong>risk</strong>: the admission
    that nothing can be perfectly defended, so protection must be prioritised by what an
    attack would cost and how likely it is. Every framework you will ever meet — NIST's,
    ISO's, a company's internal policy — is an elaboration of those two ideas. Learn them
    here and the field's enormous literature becomes variations on a theme you already know.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pleasing surprise is that the core is not technical at all. Encryption and firewalls
    are tools; the discipline is an accounting of who wants to harm which asset, through what
    weakness, at what cost to you and to them. This chapter builds that accounting.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the triad and its trade-offs" widget="equationStep" config={ciaCfg} />

  <h2>The CIA triad and risk</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Confidentiality</em> is the property that information is seen only by those meant to
    see it. It is enforced by encryption (so intercepted data is unreadable), access control
    (so the curious are kept out) and classification schemes (so everyone knows what counts
    as secret). Its failures are the headlines: leaked customer databases, intercepted
    messages, a laptop full of unencrypted patient records in a taxi.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Integrity</em> is the property that data and systems say what they are supposed to
    say — no tampering, no silent corruption, no unauthorised edits. It is enforced by
    cryptographic hashes and digital signatures (any alteration is detectable), by
    input validation (so attackers cannot rewrite queries), and by audit trails (so changes
    carry attribution). Its failures are quieter and often worse: a manipulated sensor
    reading, an altered payment, maliciously modified industrial-control logic. Note the
    asymmetry — a confidentiality breach leaks information; an integrity breach can steer
    the physical world while looking completely normal.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Availability</em> is the property that the system works when needed — the property
    outsiders most often take for granted and miss most acutely. It is engineered with
    redundancy, capacity headroom and tested backups, and attacked with denial-of-service
    floods, ransomware that encrypts everything in sight, and plain old outages. Ransomware
    is a useful case study because it attacks two properties at once: availability (your
    files are held hostage) and integrity (even restored data may not be trustworthy if the
    attackers had time to lurk).
  </p>
  <p>
    The triad's deep lesson is that the three properties trade against each other. Perfect
    confidentiality is an air-gapped computer with no network, no USB ports and no users —
    and no usefulness. Perfect availability is a system with no locks. Real security chooses
    a position, and <em>risk analysis</em> is how the position is chosen. The vocabulary:
    an <em>asset</em> is what you protect; a <em>threat</em> is who or what could harm it;
    a <em>vulnerability</em> is your weakness a threat could use; a <em>safeguard</em> is
    the control you add. Multiply the numbers and you get the classic worked form: if a
    server-room flood would cost 200,000 dollars (single loss expectancy) and is expected
    roughly once a decade (annual rate of occurrence 0.1), the annualised loss expectancy is
    20,000 dollars a year. A 5,000-dollar-a-year safeguard that meaningfully reduces that
    risk is justified; a 50,000-dollar one is not. The arithmetic is crude — probabilities
    for rare attacks are famously uncertain — but it forces the right conversation: what is
    this control <em>for</em>, and is that what it costs?
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the goal of security is to prevent all attacks. It is
    not — it is to make successful attacks rare, brief and cheap. Organisations that assume
    prevention will hold perfectly are the ones devastated when it does not; organisations
    that invest in detection and response turn a catastrophe into a bad Tuesday. "Assume
    breach" is not defeatism; it is engineering for the failure mode you cannot eliminate.
  </p>

  <h2>Threats, vulnerabilities and attacks</h2>
  <p>
    Attacks arrive through a small set of recurring doors. <em>Social engineering</em> —
    overwhelmingly phishing — attacks the human, not the machine, and industry breach
    surveys consistently find the human element involved in a large majority of incidents.
    <em>Malware</em> is code doing harm: viruses, ransomware, spyware, remote-access tools.
    <em>Exploitation</em> uses software weaknesses — injection flaws, memory bugs,
    misconfigurations — often years after they were documented and a patch was published.
    <em>Denial of service</em> attacks availability directly, drowning a service in traffic.
    And the <em>insider threat</em> walks through the front door with valid credentials,
    whether maliciously or by being fooled.
  </p>
  <p>
    Real intrusions chain several of these, which is why defenders describe them as a
    sequence (the "kill chain"): a phishing email lands; a user clicks; malware executes;
    credentials are stolen; the attacker moves laterally toward the data; exfiltration
    begins. Each link is a defensive opportunity, and the chain framing turns a vague fear
    into an engineering checklist — which links can we break, and at what cost? The same
    framing exposes a truth newcomers resist: the technique rarely matters as much as the
    preparation. Most successful attacks are not brilliant; they are patient uses of known
    weaknesses, unpatched systems and reused passwords.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> attackers are mostly genius hackers targeting victims
    one by one. In reality the bulk of crime is automated and indiscriminate: bots scan the
    entire internet continuously, credential-stuffing tools replay passwords stolen from
    one site against thousands of others, and ransomware crews buy access from specialists.
    "We're too small to matter" is the most dangerous sentence in the field — you matter to
    an algorithm that cannot tell your size.
  </p>
  <p>
    Attackers have budgets, and the defender's strategy falls straight out of theirs. Bulk
    crime — phishing campaigns, credential stuffing, commodity ransomware — is cheap,
    automated and impatient, so it abandons targets that resist and moves on; against it,
    inexpensive, broadly applied controls (patching, MFA, mail filtering) fail nearly all
    the attempts at nearly no cost. Targeted intrusion — espionage, sophisticated extortion —
    is expensive and patient: the attacker researches, waits out your defences, and accepts
    weeks of work. Against that class, prevention alone cannot be the whole answer, because
    a funded, motivated adversary eventually finds a way in. The strategy splits: make cheap
    attacks fail cheaply everywhere, and assume the expensive ones will occasionally succeed
    — which is why detection, response and rehearsed recovery are not consolation prizes but
    the second half of the defence.
  </p>

  <h2>Defence in depth, quantified</h2>
  <p>
    Since no single control is reliable, the field's structural answer is layering: phishing
    training and mail filtering, then endpoint protection, then least-privilege access and
    multi-factor authentication, then segmentation and monitoring. The reason layering works
    is arithmetic: controls compose <em>multiplicatively</em>, because an attacker must clear
    every layer in sequence. Suppose phishing gets through to one user in ten (10 percent),
    malware evades the endpoint in a quarter of cases (25 percent), and stolen credentials
    succeed against the access layer 15 percent of the time. An attacker must pass all
    three, so the success rate is 0.10 × 0.25 × 0.15 = 0.375 percent — roughly one adversary
    in 267.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="layer three controls and multiply the survivors" widget="sliderLab" config={layersCfg} />

  <p>
    Drag the sliders and notice what the multiplication teaches: improving your
    <em>weakest</em> layer pays more than perfecting your strongest, and each new layer is
    the defender's compounding interest. The caveats are equally real. Layers must be
    <em>independent</em> — three products using the same signature database are one layer
    wearing three logos. And correlated failures break the maths: if one vendor outage
    disables two "separate" controls at once, the layers were an illusion. Depth is a
    property of an architecture, not a shopping list.
  </p>

  <h2>Security vocabulary and frameworks</h2>
  <p>
    The profession coordinates itself through shared reference frames, and knowing the map
    is half of joining it. The <em>NICE Workforce Framework</em> (NIST SP 800-181) names the
    work itself — the categories of defending, analysing, operating, overseeing and building
    — and the job roles under each, which is why job postings read like its categories.
    The <em>NIST Cybersecurity Framework</em> organises any programme into six functions —
    govern, identify, protect, detect, respond and recover — a full lifecycle from boardroom
    policy to post-incident restoration (version 2.0, published in 2024, added "govern" as
    an explicit function). <em>ISO 27001</em> certifies an organisation's security management
    system: not that it is unhackable, but that it manages risk systematically. Underneath
    all of them run shared artefacts: the CVE catalogue, which gives every publicly known
    vulnerability a name and number; the CVSS scale, which scores severity from 0 to 10; and
    principles old enough to be laws — least privilege, separation of duties, defence in
    depth, and "fail securely."
  </p>
  <p>
    Two habits make the frameworks usable rather than decorative. First, map your own
    world onto them concretely: pick an asset (the customer database), walk the six CSF
    functions against it, and the gaps name themselves — no identification of that asset's
    owners, no detection tuned to queries against it, no rehearsed response if it is
    encrypted on a Sunday. Second, use the shared vocabulary in incidents: "a phishing
    delivery (threat) exploited the absence of MFA (vulnerability) to breach
    confidentiality of payroll data (asset, CIA property)" is one sentence that a board, an
    auditor and an engineer can all act on. Vagueness is expensive; the frameworks exist to
    make it inexcusable.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The triad is a model, and models leak. Practitioners add properties it underweights:
    <em>authenticity</em> (is this message really from its claimed sender?), accountability
    (can actions be attributed to a person?), and <em>non-repudiation</em> (can the sender
    later deny it?) — signatures provide these in ways plain confidentiality never can.
    Availability has its own deep literature, from redundant-array design to chaos
    engineering, and its own economics: the cost of an outage usually grows super-linearly
    with its duration, which is why the "five nines" (99.999 percent uptime, about five
    minutes of downtime a year) is a genuine design target and not a marketing flourish.
  </p>
  <p>
    The research frontier is where the interesting discomfort lives. <em>Security
    economics</em> (Ross Anderson's founding insight) shows that misaligned incentives —
    the party who can fix a vulnerability is rarely the party who bears the loss — explain
    chronic insecurity better than any technical deficit. <em>Zero-trust architecture</em>
    abandons the trusted internal network as a concept: verify every request, every time,
    which is quietly a redesign of everything. Quantum computing threatens today's public-
    key cryptography on a schedule nobody can pin down, and "harvest now, decrypt later"
    makes the threat current for long-lived secrets — hence the ongoing migration to
    post-quantum algorithms. And machine learning cuts both ways, automating phishing at
    scale while automating detection at scale. The CIA triad and risk arithmetic survive
    all of it intact — which is the final lesson: in a field where every tool ages in
    dog years, the durable curriculum is the way of thinking.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NIST SP 800-181r1 (NICE Framework); NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0; Bishop, <em>Computer Security: Art and Science</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
